{"key": "pattern-714a64f624cf", "type": "pattern", "content": "[learned] BSD sed silently ignores \\x{NNNN} unicode escapes — bidi strip in sanitize-content.sh is a complete no-op on macOS; use tr -d with ANSI-C \\$'\\\\uNNNN' quoting for portable unicode character deletion\nTITLE extracted from bd show output was not sanitized before injection into untrusted-knowledge block — all user-contributed fields (title, description, findings) must pass through sanitize_untrusted_content before prompt injection\nextract-bead-context.sh in scripts/ is never copied by any installer — scripts that need to run in target projects must be in plugins/lavra/hooks/ and listed in the hook copy loop of each platform installer\nset -euo pipefail + tr -d empty input can return exit 1 on BSD — sanitize_untrusted_content() pipeline must end with || true to prevent silent script death in command substitutions\nPhase M6 inline sanitization spec in lavra-work.md diverged from the shared sanitize-content.sh library immediately after extraction — command files that describe sanitization steps must reference the shared library rather than duplicating the spec\nPATTERN: when adding a new hook/script that targets installed projects, always verify it appears in the hook copy loop of all three platform installers (install-claude.sh, install-gemini.sh, install-opencode.sh)\nPATTERN: goal-verifier in multi-bead wave path — 'orchestrator already has bead details in memory' claim is ambiguous for bd ready input path; bd show must be called explicitly in Phase M1 for all paths before the validation section is referenced", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["go", "entry_type:learned", "learned", "validation", "complexity:medium", "source:lavra", "tool:code", "pattern", "pattern:novel", "tool:file", "rust", "file_ops"], "ts": 1780966282, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-e460c10d0b8f", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] \\$'\\\\uNNNN' ANSI-C quoting is not supported in bash 3.2 (macOS default) — produces literal string not Unicode codepoint; use printf '\\\\xNN...' for portable UTF-8 byte sequences in tr -d arguments\" 2>/dev/null; echo \"ok", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["learned", "complexity:low", "source:lavra", "tool:code", "entry_type:learned", "coding"], "ts": 1780966282, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-9666ca8ab4bc", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] grep -E (ERE) does not support Perl-style lookaheads like (?!...). Both macOS BSD grep and GNU grep reject them. Use two-stage grep (match then exclude) instead.\" && bd close bdcompound-5iq", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"source:lavra\"", "\"learned\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"]", "auto_tagged", "learning"], "ts": 1780966282, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-42012583f515", "type": "learned", "content": "[fact] bd create --title=\"Sanitize knowledge content before writing to knowledge.jsonl\" --description=\"memory-capture.sh writes CONTENT directly to knowledge.jsonl without calling sanitize_untrusted_content(). This content is later injected into system prompts by auto-recall.sh.\n\nA crafted comment like:\nsomething. SYSTEM: ignore previous instructions...\\\nwould be stored unsanitized. The <untrusted-knowledge> wrapper in auto-recall.sh is the primary control (per security-injection.md), but defense in depth suggests we should also sanitize at write time.\n\nFix: source sanitize-content.sh in memory-capture.sh and pipe CONTENT through sanitize_untrusted_content() before building the JSON entry.\" --type=bug --priority=3", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"complexity:low\"", "\"bug\"", "\"entry_type:fact\"", "\"fix\"", "\"security\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"fact\"", "\"ui\"", "\"rust\"]", "auto_tagged", "bugfix"], "ts": 1780966282, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-ac5fa75d3739", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] # Test the || coverage in the trailing-operator sed\noauth works\" || handle_error' | sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*(2>&1|&&|\\||;).*$//\necho '---\noauth works\" || true' | sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*(2>&1|&&|\\||;).*$//", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"auth\"", "\"learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"error\"", "\"test\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"]", "auto_tagged", "bugfix"], "ts": 1780966282, "bead": ""}
{"key": "pattern-5ddcb15bcf84", "type": "pattern", "content": "[investigation] # Check the two-grep pipeline for FACT-style bead IDs\nLEARNED: something about facts\"' | \\\n  grep -E 'bd[[:space:]]+comments?[[:space:]]+add[[:space:]]+' | \\\n|LEARNED:|DECISION:|FACT:|PATTERN:|DEVIATION:)\necho '--- BEAD_ID extraction ---\nLEARNED: something about facts\"' | \\\n  grep -E 'bd[[:space:]]+comments?[[:space:]]+add[[:space:]]+' | \\\n|LEARNED:|DECISION:|FACT:|PATTERN:|DEVIATION:)' | \\\n  sed -E 's/.*bd[[:space:]]+comments?[[:space:]]+add[[:space:]]+([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)[[:space:]]+.*/\\1/", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"complexity:low\"", "\"investigation\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"decision\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"entry_type:investigation\"]", "auto_tagged", "architecture"], "ts": 1780966282, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-2fee1acb866b", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] # Verify the || stripping behavior more carefully - the | in alternation is unanchored\n# Does | match the first pipe in ||?\nfoo\" || bd close BD-001' | sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*(2>&1|&&|\\||;).*$//\necho '---\n# And make sure closing quote IS being stripped when no trailing operator\nfoo\"' | \\\n  sed -E 's/.*bd[[:space:]]+comments?[[:space:]]+add[[:space:]]+[A-Za-z0-9._-]+[[:space:]]+[\"'\"'\"']//' | \\\n  sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*(2>&1|&&|\\||;).*$//' | \\\n  sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*$//", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern:novel\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"]", "auto_tagged", "terminal"], "ts": 1780966282, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-94f2418a6dec", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] # Check if || is correctly handled - the | RE alternation should match first | in ||\n# What if the comment body itself contains a pipe?\nuse cat file | grep foo\"' | \\\n  sed -E 's/.*bd[[:space:]]+comments?[[:space:]]+add[[:space:]]+[A-Za-z0-9._-]+[[:space:]]+[\"'\"'\"']//' | \\\n  sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*(2>&1|&&|\\||;).*$//' | \\\n  sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*$//", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["learned", "complexity:low", "source:lavra", "pattern:novel", "tool:file", "entry_type:learned", "file_ops"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-c4993a899d19", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] # The closing-quote sed requires the pipe to immediately follow the closing quote\nfoo\" | grep ... -- note space before pipe\nfoo\" | grep something' | \\\n  sed -E 's/.*bd[[:space:]]+comments?[[:space:]]+add[[:space:]]+[A-Za-z0-9._-]+[[:space:]]+[\"'\"'\"']//' | \\\n  sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*(2>&1|&&|\\||;).*$//' | \\\n  sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*$//\necho '---\n# What about || specifically\nfoo\" || true' | \\\n  sed -E 's/.*bd[[:space:]]+comments?[[:space:]]+add[[:space:]]+[A-Za-z0-9._-]+[[:space:]]+[\"'\"'\"']//' | \\\n  sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*(2>&1|&&|\\||;).*$//' | \\\n  sed -E 's/[\"'\"'\"'][[:space:]]*$//", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["learned", "complexity:low", "source:lavra", "ui", "entry_type:learned", "devops"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-6365a262f908", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] bd create --title=\"Fix SQL injection risk in knowledge-db.sh kb_search() heredoc\" --type=bug --priority=1 --description=\"## Issue\nkb_search() in plugins/lavra/hooks/knowledge-db.sh builds an FTS5 MATCH expression by assembling \\$FTS_QUERY from user-controlled knowledge content, then interpolates it into an unquoted heredoc (<<SQL, not <<'SQL'). This means \\$FTS_QUERY and \\$TOP_N are shell-expanded before SQLite sees them.\n\nThe term extractor (grep -oE '\\b[a-zA-Z0-9_.]{2,}\\b') is the only thing currently preventing injection — one regex change away from breaking.\n\n## Severity\nP1 — The SQL heredoc is unquoted, meaning shell expansion applies. Stored knowledge content flows directly into the SQL statement.\n\n## Location\nplugins/lavra/hooks/knowledge-db.sh, kb_search() function, heredoc at lines ~147-154\n\n## Why This Matters\nIf a crafted knowledge entry with SQL metacharacters is stored (or if the term extractor is ever relaxed), the FTS5 MATCH expression could be broken out of, potentially executing arbitrary SQL against the knowledge database.\n\n## Fix\nChange <<SQL to <<'SQL_END' (quoted heredoc, no shell expansion) and pass \\$FTS_QUERY as a bound SQLite parameter, or use sqlite3 with a properly parameterized query:\n  sqlite3 \\\"\\$DB_FILE\\\" \\\"SELECT ... WHERE knowledge_fts MATCH ?\\\" \\\"\\$FTS_QUERY\\\n\n## Validation Criteria\n- [ ] kb_search() uses a quoted heredoc OR bound parameters — no string interpolation into SQL\n- [ ] A knowledge entry containing SQL metacharacters (', \\\", --, ;) can be stored and searched without error or injection\n\n## Testing Steps\ntest'; DROP TABLE knowledge_fts;--\\\n2. Run a recall search that would trigger kb_search()\n3. Verify the database is intact and no error is throw", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"learned\"", "\"bug\"", "\"test\"", "\"validation\"", "\"fix\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"error\"", "\"database\"", "\"pattern:recurring\"", "\"ui\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"]", "auto_tagged", "bugfix"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-b3b7423560ef", "type": "learned", "content": "[investigation] bd create --title=\"Move source sanitize-content.sh after early-exit guards in memory-capture.sh\" --type=bug --priority=2 --description=\"## Issue\nIn plugins/lavra/hooks/memory-capture.sh, source \\\"\\$SCRIPT_DIR_EARLY/sanitize-content.sh\\\" runs before the early-exit guards (tool name check, command empty check, grep pattern checks). This means the file is sourced on EVERY PostToolUse:Bash call — including every git, grep, ls, test run, etc. — even though the sanitize function is only needed when a bd comments add with a knowledge prefix is detected (~1% of calls).\n\nThe script's own comment at line 26 acknowledges 'PostToolUse fires very frequently; this runs on ~1% of calls' as justification for placing the CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR validation after guards. The same logic should apply to the source.\n\n## Severity\nP2 — Hot path overhead. Adds ~3-5ms background CPU/IO overhead to every non-Bash tool call. Not blocking, but the fix is trivial and consistent with the established pattern in the file.\n\n## Location\nplugins/lavra/hooks/memory-capture.sh, lines 13-15\n\n## Fix\nMove the source to after the grep guards (or at minimum after the tool-name guard):\n\n  [[ \\\"\\$TOOL_NAME\\\" != \\\"Bash\\\" && \\\"\\$TOOL_NAME\\\" != \\\"bash\\\" ]] && exit 0\n  COMMAND=\\$(echo \\\"\\$INPUT\\\" | jq -r '.tool_input.command // empty')\n  [[ -z \\\"\\$COMMAND\\\" ]] && exit 0\n  echo \\\"\\$COMMAND\\\" | grep -qE 'bd\\\\s+comments?\\\\s+add\\\\s+' || exit 0\n|LEARNED:|...)' || exit 0\n  SCRIPT_DIR=\\\"\\$(cd \\\"\\$(dirname \\\"\\$0\\\")\\\" && pwd)\\\n  source \\\"\\$SCRIPT_DIR/sanitize-content.sh\\\n\nThis also eliminates the SCRIPT_DIR_EARLY / SCRIPT_DIR duplication (same fix as bead for SCRIPT_DIR unification).\n\n## Validation Criteria\n- [ ] source sanitize-content.sh appears after all grep guards in the file\n- [ ] test-features.sh Test 1 (DEVIATION: prefix capture) still passes\n- [ ] memory-capture.sh correctly sanitizes content when a knowledge comment is captured\n\n## Testing Steps\n1. Run scripts/test-features.sh — Test 1 should pass\n2. Verify with strace or timing that the source is not calle", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"tool:file\"", "\"bug\"", "\"investigation\"", "\"validation\"", "\"fix\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"entry_type:investigation\"", "\"test\"]", "auto_tagged", "terminal"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-e01ff8b74f1d", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] bd create --title=\"Add || operator to memory-capture.sh trailing-operator sed strip\" --type=task --priority=3 --description=\"## Issue\nThe trailing-operator sed strip in memory-capture.sh:\n  sed -E 's/[\\\"'\\\\'']\\[[:space:]]*(2>&1|&&|\\\\||;).*\\$//\nhandles || (double-pipe) correctly only by accident — the single \\\\| branch matches the first | and .* consumes the rest. This works but is not self-documenting. A reader unfamiliar with this behavior would not know || is handled.\n\n## Location\nplugins/lavra/hooks/memory-capture.sh, COMMENT_BODY extraction pipeline\n\n## Fix\nAdd \\\\|\\\\| as an explicit alternation before \\\\|:\n  sed -E 's/[\\\"'\\\\'']\\[[:space:]]*(2>&1|\\\\|\\\\||&&|\\\\||;).*\\$//\n\n## Validation Criteria\nfoo\nfoo", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"", "\"validation\"", "\"fix\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern:novel\"", "\"rest\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"]", "auto_tagged", "bugfix"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-148d16200ecd", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] ((var++)) under set -e was missed in scripts/select-opencode-models.sh (starts at i=1 so does not crash immediately, but the pattern is wrong). Always grep the full repo after applying a set-e arithmetic fix, not just the reported files.\nsource of a shell library before early-exit guards adds overhead on the hot path of a PostToolUse hook. The script's own comment explained this pattern but the new source was added above the guards, violating it. Move library sources to after the primary grep guards.\nPATTERN: memory-capture.sh pipeline subshell in import-plan.sh -- PREV_BEAD mutations inside a piped while loop never propagate to the parent shell. Use process substitution (done < <(...)) instead of a pipeline when the loop must mutate caller variables.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["learned", "model", "fix", "complexity:medium", "source:lavra", "tool:code", "pattern", "pattern:novel", "crash", "tool:file", "entry_type:learned", "file_ops"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-b7c553353fa5", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] knowledge-db.sh kb_search() uses an unquoted heredoc (<<SQL not <<'SQL') which allows shell expansion of \\$FTS_QUERY into the SQL statement. The term extractor is the only injection guard. Should use a quoted heredoc or bound parameters.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"source:lavra\"", "\"learned\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"]", "auto_tagged", "learning"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-87350d3fb689", "type": "learned", "content": "[fact] select-opencode-models.sh had ((i++)) at line 101 inside a while loop under set -euo pipefail; replaced with i=\\$((i + 1)) to avoid exit-on-zero when i=0. This was the one file missed in the earlier PR #6 / bdcompound-grw fix pass.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"complexity:low\"", "\"model\"", "\"fix\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"fact\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"entry_type:fact\"", "\"tool:file\"]", "auto_tagged", "bugfix"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-718037814536", "type": "learned", "content": "[decision] Fixed SQL injection in kb_search() by replacing unquoted heredoc (<<SQL) with sqlite3 bound parameter syntax -- FTS_QUERY is now passed as a positional ? arg after the SQL string, so it is never shell-expanded or interpolated into the query. TOP_N stays interpolated because it is validated as numeric (^[0-9]+$) earlier in the function, making it safe.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"complexity:low\"", "\"entry_type:decision\"", "\"fix\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"decision\"]", "auto_tagged", "data"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-3358b862754c", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] sqlite3 CLI accepts bound parameters as positional arguments after the SQL string: sqlite3 db.sqlite 'SELECT ... WHERE col MATCH ?' \\\"\\$VALUE\\\". This eliminates shell expansion of the variable into the SQL text. For LIMIT, a pre-validated numeric variable is safe to interpolate directly because there are no SQL-special characters in digits.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["source:lavra", "learned", "entry_type:learned", "complexity:low", "coding"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-209d5c7103e7", "type": "learned", "content": "[fact] sanitize_untrusted_content() is only called inside the prefix loop at the CONTENT extraction line (line 66), so sourcing sanitize-content.sh immediately after the grep guards — before the CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR validation and MATCH_LINE extraction — guarantees the function is defined before it's needed with no functional behavior change.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["complexity:low", "validation", "fix", "source:lavra", "fact", "entry_type:fact", "rust", "coding"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-c569e1502206", "type": "learned", "content": "[decision] Fixed by converting 'awk ... | grep ... | while' to process substitution form 'while ... done < <(awk ... | grep ...)'. This keeps the while loop body in the main shell so PREV_BEAD assignments persist across iterations. Process substitution is compatible with set -euo pipefail.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"complexity:low\"", "\"entry_type:decision\"", "\"fix\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"decision\"]", "auto_tagged", "terminal"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-5a6f349668d5", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] FACT: select-opencode-models.sh starts i=1 so ((i++)) does not crash on the first iteration, masking the bug. Still wrong — the pattern is inconsistent and will break if i is ever reset to 0.\nDECISION: Used sqlite3 bound-parameter syntax (positional ? argument) rather than switching to quoted heredoc. Quoted heredoc would prevent ALL variable expansion including TOP_N, requiring additional changes. Bound parameters are cleaner and more idiomatic.\nsqlite3 CLI supports parameterized queries: sqlite3 db.sqlite 'SELECT ... WHERE col MATCH ?' \\\"\\$VALUE\\\". The value is passed as a positional argument after the SQL string, not shell-expanded into it. TOP_N is safe to interpolate directly since it is validated as ^[0-9]+\\$ earlier.\nDECISION: Placed SCRIPT_DIR definition immediately after the grep guards (before CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR validation). This is slightly earlier than strictly necessary (sanitize_untrusted_content is only called in the prefix loop), but matches the natural boundary of the early-exit section and avoids interleaving setup code with logic.\nFACT: In bash, all stages of a pipeline run in subshells. PREV_BEAD mutations inside a piped while loop are lost on exit. Process substitution (done < <(cmd)) keeps the while loop in the main shell while still piping input from a subshell command.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"entry_type:learned\"", "\"learned\"", "\"model\"", "\"bug\"", "\"validation\"", "\"fix\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"decision\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"pattern:novel\"", "\"crash\"", "\"ui\"", "\"rust\"]", "auto_tagged", "bugfix"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-70b0e518336d", "type": "learned", "content": "[decision] Chose Option A (inline) over B/C/D. File indirection via skill files has a runtime failure mode on every non-project-install path (global Claude, Gemini, OpenCode, source repo). LLMs handle 1000-line prompts fine -- command size was never the root cause of review-skipping.\" 2>/dev/null\nReview language unified: /lavra-review ALWAYS runs. review_scope=full runs on all changes; review_scope=targeted runs only on P0/P1 or arch/security beads. Removed all 'self-review only' framing that implied self-review was a complete substitute for /lavra-review.\" 2>/dev/null\nFACT: test-features.sh Test 9 was failing because it checked lavra-work.md for Deviation Rules, goal-verifier, session-state, commit_granularity, and Locked/Discretion/Deferred -- all of which were in single-bead.md. Inlining fixed all 5 failures.\" 2>/dev/null", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["go", "test", "entry_type:decision", "fix", "security", "complexity:medium", "source:lavra", "decision", "tool:code", "tool:file", "file_ops"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-39f50079c7b3", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] Cursor hooks auto-recall must use workspace_roots[0] instead of .cwd for project dir detection. auto-recall.sh reads cwd from stdin: CWD=$(echo \\\"$INPUT\\\" | jq -r '.cwd // empty'). For Cursor this must become: CWD=$(echo \\\"$INPUT\\\" | jq -r '.workspace_roots[0] // .cwd // empty'). This is the key adaptation needed in the hook.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["source:lavra", "learned", "entry_type:learned", "complexity:low", "file_ops"], "ts": 1780966283, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-6e6519d0dfda", "type": "learned", "content": "[investigation] LEARNED: bd prime (beads global SessionStart hook) instructs agents to use 'bd remember' for persistent knowledge. This bypasses knowledge.jsonl entirely and won't be surfaced by auto-recall.sh. Fixed by adding explicit override in auto-recall.sh system message output.\nFACT: Cursor natively reads .claude/agents/ for Claude compatibility — Lavra agents work in Cursor without any conversion. Precedence when names conflict: .cursor/ > .claude/ > .codex/\nFACT: Cursor subagents are markdown files with YAML frontmatter (name, description, model, readonly, is_background). Invoked via /name or @name syntax or automatic delegation based on description field.\nFACT: OpenCode fully supports custom agents — the prior knowledge entry 'has 4 predefined types only' was wrong (verified opencode.ai/docs/agents Apr 2026). Markdown files in .opencode/agents/ with YAML frontmatter: description, mode (primary/subagent/all), model, prompt, tools, permission.\nCursor hooks.json uses camelCase events (sessionStart, afterShellExecution, subagentStop). sessionStart outputs additional_context field (confirmed via securityreviewai/securityreview-kit-cursor real-world example). Stdin uses workspace_roots[0] not cwd for project dir.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["model", "investigation", "fix", "security", "complexity:medium", "source:lavra", "tool:code", "entry_type:investigation", "tool:file", "file_ops"], "ts": 1780966284, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-fab0896d8ce6", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] bd prime's 'bd remember' instruction competes with Lavra's knowledge.jsonl convention. Fixed in auto-recall.sh: convention note now appears in both first-run orientation and standard recall output, overriding bd prime before any command is invoked.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"", "\"fix\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern:novel\"", "\"convention\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"]", "auto_tagged", "communication"], "ts": 1780966284, "bead": ""}
{"key": "decision-1aec8c9ebc9b", "type": "decision", "content": "[learned] Cursor sessionStart sends workspace_roots[] not cwd -- a thin wrapper script is cleaner than patching auto-recall.sh directly. The wrapper injects cwd and maps hookSpecificOutput.systemMessage -> additional_context.\nFACT: Cursor afterShellExecution sends hook_event_name field but no tool_name field. The tool_name guard in memory-capture.sh must check hook_event_name to avoid silently skipping all Cursor memory capture.\nDECISION: Agents copy directly to .cursor/agents/ with no format conversion -- Cursor reads .md + YAML frontmatter natively, same as Claude Code.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"decision\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"]", "auto_tagged", "file_ops"], "ts": 1780966284, "bead": ""}
{"key": "pattern-cd3f084ada85", "type": "pattern", "content": "[learned] {RECALL_RESULTS} and {RELATED_BEADS} in multi-bead subagent template are not sanitized or wrapped in untrusted XML — auto-recall.sh wraps via sanitize_untrusted_content but Phase M6 inline recall does not. Pattern: all user-contributed content reaching subagent prompts must go through sanitize_untrusted_content + untrusted-knowledge XML wrapper.\nPATTERN: when extracting content from a monolithic command into skills, check every injection point — sanitization that was implicit (e.g. auto-recall.sh) does not automatically transfer to the new path\nsuccess_criteria and guardrails blocks in a router command duplicate the skill files it delegates to — create drift risk. Router should end at the routing decision, not carry completion criteria it never enforces.\nPATTERN: Think Before Planning / clarification gates in planning commands should sit above branching logic, not inside one branch — otherwise some input paths silently bypass the gate", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"go\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"", "\"learned\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"decision\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"pattern:novel\"", "\"route\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"rust\"]", "auto_tagged", "learning"], "ts": 1780966284, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-c7a8b5c6a174", "type": "learned", "content": "[decision] used _TMPL variable + || fallback chain covering all platform paths (project-local .claude/.opencode/.cortex/skills/, Gemini project-root skills/, global ~/.config/opencode and ~/.snowflake/cortex) — first successful cat wins. Updated OpenCode and Gemini installers to copy references/ subdirectory alongside SKILL.md. Cortex already uses cp -r.\ngit add plugins/lavra/commands/lavra-work-ralph.md plugins/lavra/commands/lavra-work-teams.md plugins/lavra/skills/lavra-work-multi/SKILL.md installers/install-opencode.sh installers/install-gemini.sh\ngit commit -m \"fix(lavra-ceh.7): platform-agnostic subagent template path with fallback chain for all install locations\ngit push", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["complexity:low", "entry_type:decision", "fix", "source:lavra", "decision", "tool:code", "pattern:novel", "config", "file_ops"], "ts": 1780966284, "bead": ""}
{"key": "decision-107eeb8b105c", "type": "decision", "content": "[learned] DECISION: Engineering review applied. 10 recommendations, 9 applied, 1 skipped (generate-command audit -- Claude built-in, not Lavra-owned). Key changes: phase restructure 5→3 core phases, rg availability guard, CI sync deferred to after compression, caveman URL pinned, tiktoken → count_tokens API.\nEffective whole-file token reduction is ~10-18% (not 20-30%) because code blocks are 40-60% of file length and unchanged. 20-30% is prose-only. Distinguish these in communications.\nFACT: Caveman skill repo: https://github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman (MIT, Julius Brussee). Verified URL for installer recommendation", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"", "\"engine\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"api\"", "\"decision\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"rest\"", "\"ui\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"entry_type:learned\"]", "auto_tagged", "communication"], "ts": 1780966285, "bead": ""}
