{"key": "learned-d165131735ac", "type": "learned", "content": "[fact] bd create --title=\"Capture Astro 6 glob loader ID normalization as FACT\" --type=task --priority=3 --parent=bdcompound-7ji --description=\"## What\n\nAdd a FACT knowledge entry documenting how Astro 6's glob loader normalizes collection entry IDs.\n\n## Context\n\nDiscovered during docs sidebar fix (commit 824ca14). The sidebar was showing empty Configuration and Releases sections because the section slugs used uppercase names like 'AUTONOMOUS_EXECUTION' and 'releases/v0.7.0', but Astro 6's glob loader normalizes IDs to lowercase and also strips dots from the path (not just the .md extension).\n\nExamples:\n- AUTONOMOUS_EXECUTION.md → id: autonomous_execution\n- COST.md → id: cost\n- releases/v0.7.0.md → id: releases/v070 (dots stripped from the whole id, not just extension)\n\nThis is different from what the code comments said ('Astro 5 glob loader uses id (filename without extension)') — the dot-stripping behavior is Astro 6-specific and undocumented.\n\n## What\n\nRun this command to add the knowledge entry:\n\n\\`\\`\\`bash\nAstro 6 glob loader normalizes collection entry IDs: lowercased + dots stripped (not just .md extension). UPPERCASE.md → uppercase. releases/v0.7.0.md → releases/v070. Section slug arrays in Doc.astro and index.astro must use lowercase ids with underscores (not hyphens) matching the loader output. Discovered when sidebar showed empty Configuration/Releases sections.\\\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Decisions\n\n### Locked\n- Entry type must be FACT (hard constraint about Astro 6 behavior, not a design choice)\n- Tags should include: astro, docs, sidebar, glob-loader\n\n### Discretion\n- Wording of the entry\n\n## Testing\n- [ ] Entry appears in \\`recall.sh \\\"astro\\\"\\` output\n- [ ] Entry type is fact\n\n## Validation\n- [ ] \\`bd comments add\\` command runs without error\n- [ ] Entry is searchable via recall.sh\n\n## Files\n- .beads/memory/knowledge.jsonl (written by hook automatically)\n\n## Dependencies\nNone — standalone knowledge capture", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"decision\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"test\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"fact\"", "\"constraint\"", "\"error\"", "\"config\"", "\"entry_type:fact\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"validation\"", "\"fix\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "bugfix"], "ts": 1783128298, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-2485cc1affda", "type": "learned", "content": "[pattern] bd create --title=\"Add PATTERN entry: config/security boundary doctrine\" --type=task --priority=3 --parent=bdcompound-7ji --description=\"## What\n\nAdd a PATTERN knowledge entry consolidating the config/security boundary doctrine that was established across ~9 decision entries this week but never captured as a single searchable PATTERN.\n\n## Context\n\nThe doctrine was established across beads bdcompound-9f5, bdcompound-dnv, and others:\n- Where data lives: .beads/config/ for settings (not .beads/memory/ — merge=union corrupts YAML)\n- How agent context injection works safely: XML wrapping + role prefix sanitization + bidirectional char stripping\n- How knowledge search is done safely: grep -iF (fixed-string) not grep -i (regex — metachar injection risk)\n- Security model: anything auto-injected into agent context is untrusted, must be sanitized and tagged\n\nThe pattern is settled doctrine. It should be one searchable entry, not 9 scattered decisions.\n\n## What\n\nRun this command:\n\n\\`\\`\\`bash\nConfig/security boundary doctrine for Lavra. (1) Storage: .beads/config/ for settings, .beads/memory/ for knowledge — never mix them, merge=union in memory/ corrupts YAML frontmatter. (2) Agent context injection: always XML-wrap with <untrusted-knowledge> tag + strip role prefixes (SYSTEM:/ASSISTANT:/[INST]) + strip bidirectional override chars + strip null bytes before injection. (3) Safe search: grep -iF (fixed-string) not grep -i — prevents metacharacter injection from knowledge content. Applies to: auto-recall.sh, any new hook that reads .beads/ content.\\\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Decisions\n\n### Locked\n- Entry type must be PATTERN\n- Must cover all three aspects: storage separation, injection sanitization, safe search\n\n### Discretion\n- Exact wording\n\n## Testing\n- [ ] \\`recall.sh \\\"security boundary\\\"\\` returns this entry\n- [ ] \\`recall.sh \\\"config memory\\\"\\` returns this entry\n- [ ] Entry type is pattern\n\n## Validation\n- [ ] Single entry replaces the need to search 9 decision entries for this doctrine\n- [ ] Tags include: security, config, p", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"decision\"", "\"security\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"pattern:novel\"", "\"test\"", "\"entry_type:pattern\"", "\"config\"", "\"rust\"", "\"model\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"validation\"", "\"fix\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "file_ops"], "ts": 1783128298, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-1dca638c3267", "type": "learned", "content": "[investigation] bd create --title=\"Fix PATTERN type gap: add guidance to use PATTERN: prefix explicitly\" --type=task --priority=4 --parent=bdcompound-7ji --description=\"## What\n\nAdd a PATTERN knowledge entry (and update CLAUDE.md guidance if appropriate) about when to use PATTERN: type explicitly vs burying patterns in DECISION/LEARNED entries.\n\n## Context\n\nThe retro found 0 explicit PATTERN: type entries this week despite 7 entries auto-tagged 'pattern' from content. The tags come from keyword detection in memory-capture.sh, but the entry type stayed 'decision' or 'learned'. This makes it harder to filter patterns specifically and defeats the purpose of having distinct knowledge types.\n\nThe six types each have a purpose:\n- LEARNED: insight from debugging/surprise behavior — one-time discovery\n- DECISION: what was chosen and why — specific choice with rationale\n- FACT: hard constraint, version req, environment detail — static truth\n- PATTERN: recurring convention or idiom — should be reused everywhere\nroot cause analysis\n- DEVIATION: auto-fix outside bead scope\n\nWhen something is worth reusing as a convention (not just recording a one-time choice), it should be PATTERN.\n\n## What\n\n1. Add this as a PATTERN entry itself:\n\n\\`\\`\\`bash\nbd comments add bdcompound-7ji \\\"PATTERN: Use PATTERN: type when capturing something meant to be reused as a convention — not just recording a one-time decision. Signal: if you'd want a future agent to do the same thing by default, it's a PATTERN. If you're recording why you made a specific choice, it's a DECISION. Examples of PATTERN: 'always XML-wrap injected content', 'use grep -iF for safe search'. Examples of DECISION: 'chose PostgreSQL over SQLite for this project', 'dropped filesystem scanning due to security risk'.\\\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n2. Optionally update the knowledge type descriptions in CLAUDE.md or hooks-system.md rule to add a 'When to use PATTERN vs DECISION' note.\n\n## Decisions\n\n### Locked\n- The knowledge entry itself must use PATTERN: type (meta-example)\n\n### Discretion\n- Whether to also ", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"env\"", "\"decision\"", "\"security\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"postgres\"", "\"pattern:recurring\"", "\"ui\"", "\"convention\"", "\"bug\"", "\"rationale\"", "\"investigation\"", "\"constraint\"", "\"entry_type:investigation\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"fix\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "bugfix"], "ts": 1783128298, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-a80fa2d184e2", "type": "learned", "content": "[fact] bd create --title=\"Document chore: commit convention for infra/config changes\" --type=task --priority=4 --parent=bdcompound-7ji --description=\"## What\n\nAdd a knowledge entry and optionally a CLAUDE.md note clarifying when to use chore: vs other conventional commit prefixes, specifically for the infra/config changes that tend to slip through as untyped.\n\n## Context\n\nThe CNAME commit this week was untyped: 'add CNAME for lavra.dev custom domain' should have been 'chore: add CNAME for lavra.dev custom domain'. Low stakes individually, but untyped commits break changelog generation and git log --grep filtering.\n\nThe ambiguous cases are:\n- DNS/domain changes → chore:\n- CI config changes → chore: or ci: (either fine, ci: preferred)\n- Dependency bumps → chore:\n- Gitignore/gitattributes → chore:\n- README-only → docs:\n- Script/tool additions → chore: (if not user-facing) or feat: (if user-facing)\n\n## What\n\nAdd a FACT entry:\n\n\\`\\`\\`bash\nConventional commit type for infra/config-only changes: use chore: for DNS records, CI config, gitignore, gitattributes, dependency bumps, non-user-facing scripts. Use ci: for CI-specific changes (either is fine but ci: is more discoverable). These are the cases most likely to slip through as untyped commits.\\\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nOptionally add a one-liner to CLAUDE.md global instructions if the untyped pattern recurs.\n\n## Decisions\n\n### Locked\n- Entry type: FACT (concrete rule, not a design choice)\n\n### Discretion\n- Whether to add to CLAUDE.md (only worth it if this is a recurring mistake)\n\n## Testing\n- [ ] Entry searchable via \\`recall.sh \\\"chore commit\\\"\\`\n\n## Validation\n- [ ] Tags include: git, commit, convention, chore\n\n## Files\n- .beads/memory/knowledge.jsonl\n- ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (optional)\n\n## Dependencies\nNone\" --priority=4", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"decision\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"pattern:recurring\"", "\"convention\"", "\"test\"", "\"fact\"", "\"config\"", "\"entry_type:fact\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"validation\"", "\"fix\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "file_ops"], "ts": 1783128298, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-7eaea83be499", "type": "learned", "content": "[learned] When grepping CATALOG.md for command names in pre-release checks, constrain to table rows only (lines starting with |). Prose descriptions reference command names inline (e.g., 'Included in /lavra-design') and produce false positives if the whole document is searched.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"entry_type:learned\"", "\"complexity:low\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"learned\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "research"], "ts": 1783128298, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-d139020ff4c5", "type": "learned", "content": "[pattern] Config/security boundary doctrine for Lavra. (1) Storage separation: .beads/config/ for settings, .beads/memory/ for knowledge — never mix them. merge=union in memory/ is for conflict-free JSONL merging; applying it to YAML/JSON config files corrupts frontmatter. (2) Agent context injection: always XML-wrap injected content with <untrusted-knowledge> tag + strip role prefixes (SYSTEM:/ASSISTANT:/[INST]) + strip Unicode bidirectional override chars (U+202A-U+202E, U+2066-U+2069) + strip null bytes before injection into system messages. (3) Safe search: use grep -iF (fixed-string, case-insensitive) not grep -i — prevents regex metacharacter injection when search terms come from bead titles or other user-controlled content. Known limitation: bidi-strip sed uses codepoint escapes that are not portable across all sed implementations — best-effort, not guaranteed. Newline-in-JSON gap: SANITIZED_KNOWLEDGE content is not newline-escaped before JSON embedding (unlike ESCAPED_STATE), so multi-line entries are best-effort safety only. Applies to: auto-recall.sh, any new hook reading .beads/ content into agent context.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"security\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"pattern:novel\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"entry_type:pattern\"", "\"config\"", "\"rust\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"fix\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "file_ops"], "ts": 1783128299, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-263cb63c2a41", "type": "learned", "content": "[pattern] type when capturing something meant to be reused as a convention — not just recording a one-time decision. The clearest signal: can you write it as a rule? 'When doing A, do B' = PATTERN. 'We chose A over B in this case because of C' = DECISION. LEARNED vs PATTERN overlap: LEARNED is for one-time discoveries that surprised you, not rules you would apply again. If you are filing a naming convention or architectural constraint as LEARNED, it is probably a PATTERN. Borderline example from this codebase: 'use beads- prefix for all workflow commands' is a PATTERN (a rule to follow every time). 'Renamed /deepen-plan to /beads-deepen for consistency' is a DECISION (a specific application of that pattern). One PATTERN entry replaces the need for many scattered DECISION entries that all reference the same underlying convention. This entry is itself a meta-example: it describes when to use PATTERN, and is filed as PATTERN so it is recalled when classifying future knowledge entries.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"decision\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"pattern:recurring\"", "\"convention\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"constraint\"", "\"entry_type:pattern\"", "\"rest\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"fix\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "file_ops"], "ts": 1783128299, "bead": ""}
{"key": "decision-63a674119069", "type": "decision", "content": "[investigation] Inventory complete. Lavra writes to 3 .beads/ subdirs: .beads/memory/ (knowledge.jsonl, recall.sh, knowledge-db.sh, session-state.md, .lavra-version, SQLite cache), .beads/config/ (lavra.json, project-setup.md, codebase-profile.md), .beads/retros/ (date.json snapshots). Hooks (auto-recall.sh, memory-capture.sh, provision-memory.sh) have hardcoded .beads/ paths throughout. Commands reference .beads/memory/ and .beads/config/ in their markdown bodies. Uninstaller does NOT clean up .beads/ data by design (preserves knowledge).\n\nFACT: beads backup/ dir (.beads/backup/) is owned by the beads CLI, not Lavra. Migration must not touch it.\n\nDECISION: Folder-level .lavra/.gitignore and .lavra/.gitattributes chosen over subdirectory-level. Cleaner — one file controls all ignore/merge rules for the directory. Previously .beads/memory/ had its own .gitattributes and .gitignore which was fragmented.\n\nDECISION: Hard recode .beads/ → .lavra/ (no dual-path fallback, no LAVRA_DIR env var). Migration script handles existing installs. Simpler hooks = fewer bugs.\n\nDECISION: Auto-migrate on install: copy + warn. Copy .beads/{memory,config,retros} to .lavra/. Leave .beads/ originals. Print warning: 'Old Lavra data preserved in .beads/ — safe to delete after verification'. No interactive prompt, no auto-delete.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"env\"", "\"decision\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"cache\"", "\"bug\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"investigation\"", "\"entry_type:investigation\"", "\"config\"", "\"migration\"", "\"complexity:medium\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "file_ops"], "ts": 1783128299, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-f1963ed5d7fe", "type": "learned", "content": "[fact] .gitattributes and .gitignore move to .lavra/ root (not .lavra/memory/). Paths inside them use memory/ prefix: 'memory/knowledge.jsonl merge=union', 'memory/knowledge.db'. This is the key structural difference from the old setup.\n\n.lavra-version marker moves to .lavra/.lavra-version (root of .lavra/), not .lavra/memory/.lavra-version. auto-recall.sh must be updated to match in Phase 2.\n\nsubagent-wrapup.sh and knowledge-db.sh need ZERO changes. knowledge-db.sh is fully path-agnostic (all paths passed as parameters). subagent-wrapup.sh has no .beads/ refs.\n\n.lavra-version is at .lavra/.lavra-version (root), not .lavra/memory/. auto-recall.sh needs a LAVRA_DIR variable separate from MEMORY_DIR to read the version file correctly.\n\nPATTERN: Use cp -rn (no-clobber recursive) for migration to avoid overwriting if .lavra/ already exists. Migration must be idempotent.\n\n.beads/backup/ in lavra-ship.md must NOT be changed — it is owned by the beads CLI, not Lavra. All other .beads/ refs in commands should be updated.\n\nreleases/v0.7.0.md and other historical release notes must NOT be updated. They document what was true at that version. Only update current/active docs.\n\n772 total .beads/ references found across ~50 files. After all phases, final grep check should find zero non-exception results. Exceptions: .beads/backup/ in lavra-ship.md, migration detection code in installers, releases/v*.md historical notes.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"tool:file\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"fact\"", "\"entry_type:fact\"", "\"migration\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"fix\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "file_ops"], "ts": 1783128299, "bead": ""}
{"key": "learned-05d6ffaabf35", "type": "learned", "content": "[investigation] Plan deepened with research from 7 agents (architecture, security, simplicity, deployment, data-migration, goal-verifier, performance). Key improvements: (1) Platform copies (opencode/, gemini/, cortex/) added to Phase 4 scope — 82+ files missed in original plan. (2) Migration function hardened: TARGET validation, SQLite exclusion, stale .gitattributes/.gitignore exclusion, post-copy line count check, idempotent re-run protection. (3) Migration smoke test (upgrade scenario) added to Phase 6. (4) check-memory.sh fallback hardcode at line 97 identified as easily-missed critical fix in Phase 2.\n\nFACT: cp -rn destination behavior: if destination dir does NOT exist, copies contents. If it DOES exist, copies into it. mkdir -p for .lavra/retros/ MUST precede cp -rn for retros migration. This is a known cp quirk that causes wrong-path bugs.\n\nFACT: Old .beads/memory/.gitattributes and .beads/memory/.gitignore MUST be excluded from migration copy. If they land in .lavra/memory/, they create redundant subdirectory-level attribute/ignore files that conflict with the new .lavra/ root-level files. Exclude them explicitly in the migration loop.\n\nPATTERN: git check-attr merge .lavra/memory/knowledge.jsonl is the correct post-install verification for merge=union. Must be in Phase 6 smoke test checklist.\n\nDECISION: Version 0.8.0 (minor bump). Structural directory change with migration is significant enough for minor version. Not a patch.", "source": "knowledge-cube", "tags": ["[\"ui\"", "\"complexity:medium\"", "\"decision\"", "\"tool:file\"", "\"go\"", "\"pattern:novel\"", "\"migration\"", "\"deploy\"", "\"test\"", "\"architecture\"", "\"security\"", "\"source:lavra\"", "\"pattern\"", "\"performance\"", "\"bug\"", "\"tool:code\"", "\"investigation\"", "\"entry_type:investigation\"", "\"validation\"", "\"fix\"]", "auto_tagged", "auto_tagged", "file_ops"], "ts": 1783128299, "bead": ""}
